  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_f8eee7b4d94d405e8ae1cd7638c1d8cf",
      "statement": "ZeroHubClient.reconnect handles the 1001 going-away redirect case (newHost argument) before falling back to host failover: the close reason is validated as a well-formed host via isWellFormedHost (client/src/utils.ts, mirroring the server's validateBackupHost); a malformed reason logs a warning and falls through to the automatic host-failover path without touching host state; a redirect equal to this.host (self-redirect) surfaces onZeroHubError and stops; a valid redirect updates this.host and hostIndex (pointing at the redirected host when it is in the configured list), resets the same-host retry budget and sweep round, and re-dials.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://client/src/zeroHub.ts#L538-L621",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a9b3204bb3d3114647cbf6494a66eea38f41280745b86c9047f7407fa478292a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_4b0dc7e91368409180c96e9a923cf9e7",
      "statement": "ZeroHubClient.disconnect() sets isDisconnected, clears pending ICE and P2P-recovery timers, calls the shared closeActiveSocket() helper (which detaches onclose/onerror/onmessage/onopen and closes the socket with code 1000 so onclose does not auto-reconnect), then transitions every peer to Disconnected and closes each RTCPeerConnection via removePeer, emptying the peers map; the client is reusable afterwards. connectToZeroHub also calls closeActiveSocket() at the top to tear down any socket left open by a previous entry-point call without going through disconnect(), nulling handlers first so the replaced socket does not fire reconnect() (which would advance hostIndex a second time for a single logical failure) or onZeroHubError.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://client/src/zeroHub.ts#L1165-L1176",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0f9b8246a41893c3af6043a7d7529ebfce115acbf7614b50da89621fe6b49b81:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://client/src/zeroHub.ts#L1388-L1425",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:1c458d9a9b9f8a30f4b18553b19f13ba72e56644542b2d249b1c387a1092b36f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_82a5551e89604d8aaaa4f2c8a8c72c42",
      "statement": "The WebSocket onclose handler interprets close codes: 1000 (normal close) does nothing; 1001 (going away) hands the close reason to reconnect(url, reason) as the migration redirect; 1006/1011/1012/1013 (abnormal closure, internal error, service restart, try-again-later — all transient codes) call reconnect(url) without settling the connect Promise; any other code is a protocol-level failure that fires onZeroHubError and rejects the connect Promise.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://client/src/zeroHub.ts#L1319-L1368",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:d36000e1e9fe52d95945f74e83e13d52ef3f579d85b29b1786d35c83bc092c4f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_bd4128b0cd9d456c8ec9c4355a36c977",
      "statement": "SFUTopology routes all P2P traffic through a single SFU peer (the numerically lowest peer ID by default, or an explicitly set one — peer IDs are sequential decimal strings, so election sorts parseInt(id, 10) rather than lexicographically); the SFU offers to every client and non-SFU peers connect only to the SFU, trading a single point of failure for better large-group scalability. P2P drops are recovered only from the SFU side by re-sending an offer; an explicitly configured SFU is never re-elected.",
