    {
      "id": "claim_2b6a3e2ef22b4cc387020205c3a3370c",
      "statement": "The WebSocket onerror handler logs and fires onZeroHubError but does not call reconnect: per RFC 6455 a network failure fires error and then close with code 1006, and reconnecting in both handlers would advance hostIndex twice for a single failure (skipping two hosts); all reconnection is handled in onclose. onerror also settles the connect Promise for a dial that failed before it ever opened.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://client/src/zeroHub.ts#L1308-L1318",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:fb832b28237a4323a2780081ef33136358bc8e4fefd1222d33edbcea4540bef4:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3d7994bff5cc4ef1aa3bd7aaf510d9a3",
      "statement": "All four hub connection entrypoints (createRandomHub, joinRandomHub, joinStaticHub, joinOrCreateIPHub) funnel into the private openHubConnection helper, which stores any supplied metadata, builds a URL from getWS(host, tls) with id/hubMetadata/peerMetadata query params as applicable, and calls connectToZeroHub. The former createHub/joinHub/joinOrCreateHub (named-hub) and createPermanentHub/joinPermanentHub entrypoints no longer exist — named hubs are now 'static hubs' whose creation is an admin-gated plain-HTTP endpoint — and joinIPHub was removed because a client-supplied id can never resolve to an IP hub.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://client/src/zeroHub.ts#L1812-L1913",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0edd2cf6792b4f7b4438e3c69d1032162355429c19ee4ac65231d78726abbc91:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_94dec8e87c65413b9d94fa60a17902c9",
      "statement": "On a hubInfoMessage the client reconciles its local peer roster against the authoritative roster carried in the message: it adds any roster peer not yet in the map and prunes any peer it still holds that is no longer listed and is not itself, firing ZeroHubDisconnected and tearing the peer down via the shared removePeer helper. This prune pass is the auto-reconnect fix; without it a peer that left the hub while the socket was down would linger as a stale Peer holding a live, unclosed RTCPeerConnection.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://client/src/zeroHub.ts#L804-L839",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:d3d0b505365f1fcf569ad0e1ce62d17196b3cf4e497b4e748839f64e935777da:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_f2fc909679c64c51b575825c7dc469b9",
      "statement": "Hub and peer metadata received from the server is parsed through the module-level safeParseJson(raw, fallback) helper, which returns the parsed JSON on success or the fallback when the input is missing or malformed; the caller logs a warning only on a genuine parse failure, so a corrupt metadata value from the server degrades to the fallback instead of throwing and crashing the WebSocket message dispatcher.",
      "evidence": [
